#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494f9793-de3b-5a0e-8ee9-38a8bad0f970" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dccf38bb-e173-596a-a5e6-40e2594426b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357340} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2b9737-4208-54a4-9641-00ec2aed40cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357341} {:answer/id #uuid "a80e0f67-361c-5a78-886b-0c8034ed8c0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357342} {:answer/id #uuid "a62cfca6-edc1-51c2-865e-f477b4e6129c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357343} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8705fc-991a-530c-a9e0-d45dc8ea6831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a62cfca6-edc1-51c2-865e-f477b4e6129c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357343} :answer-field/id #uuid "39104662-f3eb-59ae-b338-4ddf553f285d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa5fba2b-b9cd-5f9f-94be-1d958a9dbda2" :question/math-academy-id "q-106623" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6e2d190-586a-5283-a92b-ca666e36d4a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357347} {:answer/id #uuid "40b74dce-1d22-59f8-8e8f-0eb11a7da9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357348} {:answer/id #uuid "b876ae21-e4ff-57da-a25c-af8d22e1a520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [0,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357349} {:answer/id #uuid "524aff31-cbe2-5d1f-bc59-77a837d8d7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357350} {:answer/id #uuid "4764fa15-f1ec-529c-b513-ed3ac74da8ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6e2d190-586a-5283-a92b-ca666e36d4a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357347} :answer-field/id #uuid "f14aa825-c4f3-5e54-9cd2-d57d683c2d15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4002f7a8-11ca-5268-8529-2e1c2c8cfac5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106685" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/assets/32375f839dd0f51a8624bebda732d7413130c940045db7e5a1a9fce3576e91b6.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/assets/d44195c764b2943c3d5b5e0d91ee770011c951d7a825f0c5cc4d1f38cedbb709.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the upper branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the upper branch are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57897eda-42bc-5dba-b320-d8a3d9ba2188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357354} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7a1b71-fc81-5788-8fb2-bac3e38283bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357355} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf8bf47-2c56-53ce-8177-0d9d7e78d12b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [0,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357356} {:answer/id #uuid "38248df6-45d9-5619-a06e-bc2bfaeec2e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357357} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1cf1d2-8c03-578f-be36-af20697303b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57897eda-42bc-5dba-b320-d8a3d9ba2188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357354} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3bdd91e-1ea6-5d0a-be55-5e94695fd3c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6469c7a5-493e-5e62-84eb-8c3dac67cf01" :question/math-academy-id "q-106687" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/assets/1474fb8efcada2aec61c1aa2b2ed16615e0ba968ec4e53a8fbfe05b1107c085f.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/assets/46c9d9139a3cff2a76871c69deeac60492d2c9aef4bf1f7ac07a16868d31d6e0.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the upper branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the upper branch are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e538c21e-67fd-56f7-b09f-a9fec3121f78" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d756cb5-6205-50b4-9906-1773121f4181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357361} {:answer/id #uuid "e2fb28a3-30b3-585a-ac2a-da68e3518dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357362} {:answer/id #uuid "bd90b2ee-e5a1-55cd-baab-6cfa555b27d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357363} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7644ce-84a8-50f6-abcf-155eb4c75093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357364} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ecabb7-93b2-50b8-af3f-06d91b6d9bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b7644ce-84a8-50f6-abcf-155eb4c75093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357364} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ec82df7-cd38-5ee8-b925-b8a85fac3bb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8a0ddc8-972a-5f01-829a-2b6930d2f171" :question/math-academy-id "q-89768" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/assets/432f8963425031983284db54310c8ae604bc993de66494dceb48e6a4e2bcdf73.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{7}{6}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,-7)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=7.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{7}{6}x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{b} & =\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ b & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e538c21e-67fd-56f7-b09f-a9fec3121f78" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86890b6b-a375-5288-80c1-558cf05a16f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357368} {:answer/id #uuid "2c2031c0-4714-58b5-a884-790c330852b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357369} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5e176a-5c1f-5418-9ad6-89bc3ec7de53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357370} {:answer/id #uuid "db033769-6d7e-5417-b27f-49279bb7b70c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357371} {:answer/id #uuid "40b62951-4ea5-5183-bf6e-bb1af026a536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c2031c0-4714-58b5-a884-790c330852b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357369} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce62ac01-b4cc-582a-90f0-d5878cb421c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e45c70e0-74a6-58a6-9db9-63d038c10666" :question/math-academy-id "q-89774" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068362/assets/245037d572c2bc9c81d3cdfb7a485b167c15223c68e3dbb8e40eaa2cc7650da9.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm x.$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,-1)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=1.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{b} & =1 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"}]}
